INT. INTERROGATION ROOM, PRECINCT 9 - NIGHT

A steel table, two chairs, a one-way mirror that has seen better decades. A single fluorescent tube buzzes. A tape recorder sits between them, its reels still.

DETECTIVE JOSEPHINE "JO" PRUITT, forty-five, shirtsleeves rolled, leans back in her chair with a coffee. Across from her sits CARMINE LUCIANO, fifty, in a camel overcoat, his hands folded, a gold ring on every finger.

JO
Mr. Luciano. Thanks for coming in.

CARMINE
I came in because you asked nicely.

JO
I always ask nicely.

CARMINE
That's what worries me.

She sets her coffee down. She switches on the tape recorder. The reels begin to turn.

JO
For the record, state your whereabouts on Saturday night.

CARMINE
Home. With my wife.

JO
Your wife is in Palm Beach.

CARMINE
Then I was home alone.

JO
Doing what?

CARMINE
Reading.

JO
What were you reading?

CARMINE
A cookbook.

She smiles. He doesn't. She slides a photograph across the table. He glances at it, then back at her face.

JO
Do you know this man?

CARMINE
(a pause)
No.

JO
His name was Anthony Bellavance. He was found in the river.

CARMINE
A tragedy.

JO
He was your accountant.

CARMINE
I have many accountants.

JO
This one kept two sets of books.

Carmine's jaw tightens. He unfolds his hands, places them flat on the table, and examines the rings.

CARMINE
Detective, are you charging me?

JO
Not yet.

CARMINE
Then I'd like my lawyer.

JO
He's on his way.

CARMINE
Good.

JO
Good.

CARMINE ^
You're making a mistake.

JO
You're making one too.

A long silence. The fluorescent tube buzzes. Carmine takes a cigar from his coat. She watches him. He sniffs it, lets it hang in his fingers.

CARMINE
May I?

JO
No smoking.

CARMINE
Even for old friends?

JO
We're not friends.

CARMINE
You wound me.

JO
I'd rather arrest you.

He laughs, genuinely, and puts the cigar away.

CARMINE
You remind me of someone.

JO
Who?

CARMINE
Your father.

Her coffee cup stops halfway to her mouth. She sets it down slowly, deliberately, without a sound.

JO
Say that again.

CARMINE
I knew Mickey Pruitt. A good cop. A hard man. We had an understanding.

JO
He never took a dime.

CARMINE
No. He took something better.

JO
What?

CARMINE
My word.

Jo rises. The chair legs scrape. She walks to the mirror and stands with her back to him, her palms against the glass. In the reflection her face is steady and her hands are not.

JO
(not turning)
What did he ask for?

CARMINE
He asked me to leave his daughter out of it.

JO
Out of what?

CARMINE
Out of everything.

She turns. For the first time, Carmine looks tired. He folds his hands again and waits. The tape recorder turns.

JO
I need a minute.

CARMINE
Take two.

She crosses to the door. She opens it, pauses, and glances back.

JO
Don't go anywhere.

CARMINE
Where would I go?

She leaves. The door closes. He leans back and studies the mirror, and then, very slightly, he nods at it, as though at an old friend on the other side.
